@startuml ContainerEngineDetection
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' Destination diagram — prose from internal/engine/engine.go and the docker
' preflight in cmd/proveo/provision.go lands here.
title Container engine detection — name the engine, never assume Docker Desktop

node "internal/engine" as ENG <<host>> {
  component "DetectOffline()\ncontext endpoint · engine CLI version" as OFF <<app>>
  component "Detect()\nDetectOffline + daemon confirmation" as DET <<app>>
  component "classify(context, endpoint)\nsocket path first, context name second" as CLS <<app>>
  component "classifyInfo(OperatingSystem, Name)\ndaemon self-description" as CINF <<app>>
  component "profiles table\nname · start · versionCLI\nsockets · contexts · infoOS" as PROF <<app>>
}

database "active docker context\nDOCKER_HOST, else\ndocker context inspect" as CTX <<db>>
database "docker info\nOperatingSystem · Name · ServerVersion" as INFO <<db>>

component "sbx-unavailable warn\ncmd/proveo run.Do" as WARN <<app>>
component "docker preflight\nensureDockerUsable" as PRE <<app>>

OFF ARROW_MAIN CTX
OFF ARROW_MAIN CLS
CLS ARROW_OPTIONAL PROF
DET ARROW_MAIN OFF
DET ARROW_MAIN INFO
DET ARROW_OPTIONAL CINF
WARN ARROW_MAIN DET
PRE ARROW_MAIN OFF

note right of CLS
  **Socket before context, and both before the daemon.** The endpoint socket
  is matched first because that answer survives a //stopped// daemon — which is
  exactly the moment a "start it" hint is worth printing. The context name is
  the second-best signal; ""docker info"" only refines a guess the socket could
  not settle, such as a hand-rolled ""DOCKER_HOST"".

  The generic ""/var/run/docker\.sock"" is claimed only on Linux: on macOS that
  path is almost always a symlink some engine installed, so matching it there
  would mislabel every host.
end note

note bottom of PROF
  **Why detection exists at all.** macOS is a field of alternatives — OrbStack,
  Colima, Rancher Desktop, Podman, Lima — and Docker Desktop is one option among
  them, not the default. A hint that says "start Docker Desktop" to an OrbStack
  user is worse than no hint: it names software they deliberately removed.

  Version comes from the engine's own CLI where one exists, so a host reads
  ""OrbStack 2.2.3"" — the thing the user installed — rather than ""29.4.0"", the
  docker server it happens to bundle.
end note

note left of PRE
  ""ensureDockerUsable"" runs ""DetectOffline"" on purpose: it is called only
  //after// ""docker info"" has already failed, so re-running it would be both
  redundant and the one call that can block on a dead socket.
end note

note bottom of DET
  **No detectable engine is a microVM boundary.** Every one of these runs
  containers on a single shared Linux kernel; OrbStack's "isolated machines"
  cut off host and file access but are explicitly //not// a VM boundary. So the
  engine is a **naming** concern, never a backend choice — nothing here can
  stand in for ""sbx"" (own kernel per sandbox, see
  ""_spec/internal/sbx/sandbox-backend\.puml""), and ""sbx"" in turn needs no
  docker engine at all. That is why the sbx-unavailable warning names the
  engine //and// says the install is standalone: read without it, "sbx CLI not
  found" is taken as "reinstall Docker Desktop".
end note

@enduml
